wordfreq frequency table (data/wordfreq/, src/wordfreq.ts)
----------------------------------------------------------
The word-frequency table in this repository is derived from

  wordfreq — https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq
  Robyn Speer and contributors

converted by scripts/import-wordfreq.py from the installed wordfreq
package (version 3.1.1): the English "best" word list, trimmed to
words at Zipf frequency 2.0 or above (95,641 words), each word with
its Zipf value. The wordfreq CODE is under the Apache License 2.0
(version 3.x; earlier releases MIT). The frequency DATA files are
redistributable under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike
4.0 International License (CC BY-SA 4.0), per the wordfreq
documentation: https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq — the data
redistributes the wordfreq contributors' work and data from
multiple sources, including the SUBTLEX frequency lists (Marc
Brysbaert and colleagues), whose credit the wordfreq documentation
asks redistributors to carry; it is carried here. ShareAlike
applies to the table data: redistributions of the table, modified
or not, carry CC BY-SA 4.0.

The table is descriptive knowledge about words' currency, read by
the NormalcyUnit (src/ansci/normalcy-unit.ts) as labelled facts;
no value in it sets a price, a threshold, or a reply anywhere in
the system.

The Glasgow Norms (data/glasgow/, src/glasgow.ts)
------------------------------------------------
The Glasgow Norms table in this repository is the dataset of

  Scott, G. G., Keitel, A., Becirspahic, M., Yao, B., & Sereno,
  S. C. (2019). The Glasgow Norms: A repository of semantic,
  lexical and phonological norms for 5,553 English words.
  Behavior Research Methods, 51, 1258–1270.
  https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-018-1099-3

converted by scripts/import-glasgow.py from the article's
supplementary material (ESM 2), distributed with the article
under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License
(CC BY 4.0): https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ — the
article is Open Access under CC BY 4.0, and the norms are its
supplementary material. The WHOLE table is imported: 5,553 words
× nine dimensions (arousal, valence, dominance, concreteness,
imageability, familiarity, age of acquisition, semantic size,
gender association), each dimension a mean with its standard
deviation and rater count.

The norms are descriptive knowledge about words, read by the
NormalcyUnit (src/ansci/normalcy-unit.ts) as labelled facts with
their provenance. Familiarity serves NormalcyAssessment in this
arc; the other dimensions are prepared as labelled access for
their consumer units under the same rule — no value in the table
sets a price, fires a flag, or decides a reply anywhere in the
system. Gender association is guarded knowledge: its values
change no price, flag, referent or pronoun decision, or reply
in v1 (pinned by test).

Everything else in this repository — the ontology graph, seed data, reasoner
and chat — is original AnSci work (see README.md). ConceptNet is a planned
base net (design doc §4) and is not yet bundled; when it lands, its
attribution will be added here.
